# Unit 1: Electrochemistry and Applications

## Electrodes and Electrode Potential

### Electrodes

Electrodes are the foundational building blocks of electrochemical cells and batteries.

### Electrode Potential

It is the tendency of an electrode to lose or gain electrons when it is in contact with a solution of its own ions.

### Types of Electrodes

- **Anode:** Electrode at which oxidation takes place.
- **Cathode:** Electrode at which reduction takes place.

### Determination of Electrode Potential by Calomel Electrode

Calomel electrode is used as a reference electrode to determine electrode potential of an unknown electrode.

### Construction

Calomel electrode consists of mercury, mercurous chloride paste and potassium chloride solution.

### Electrode Representation

Hg | Hg₂Cl₂ | KCl

### Electrode Reaction

Hg₂Cl₂ + 2e⁻ → 2Hg + 2Cl⁻

### Working

Calomel electrode is known as mercury-mercurous chloride electrode. The potential of this electrode depends on the KCl solution used.

